# The three-valued X <= Y of balls with 2-limb centers (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface",
# "Comparisons": certain): with the bounds of ball_less_2, yes when s <= dl, s and dl finite; no
# when s < ml, ml finite. Each implies its relation; never both. Both false for an empty operand and
# for the whole line. Certain, not exact.
kernel:        ball_less_equal_2
input:         cx = cx0 cx1
input:         stored rx
input:         cy = cy0 cy1
input:         stored ry
output:        mask yes
output:        mask no
requires:      ball cx rx
requires:      ball cy ry
ensures:       trit yes no le x y x=cx,rx y=cy,ry
min_precision: 3
source:        a bound of the sum of the radii by radd and bounds of the difference of the centers by add_ball_2_2, compared (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the results are masks, which have no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; trit states what comes out for every canonical ball

ncx0        = neg(cx0)
ncx1        = neg(cx1)
(z0, z1, e) = add_ball_2_2(cy0, cy1, ncx0, ncx1)
s           = radd(rx, ry)
sa          = abs(s)
fs          = lt(sa, const inf)
zd          = to_down_2(z0, z1)
nzd         = neg(zd)
tl          = add_up_1(e, nzd)
dl          = neg(tl)
la          = abs(dl)
fl          = lt(la, const inf)
zu          = to_up_2(z0, z1)
tm          = add_up_1(zu, e)
ml          = neg(tm)
ma          = abs(ml)
fm          = lt(ma, const inf)
a           = le(s, dl)
ys          = and(a, fs)
yes         = and(ys, fl)
b           = lt(s, ml)
no          = and(b, fm)
